pseudo elements there's more to your  HTML than meets the eye there are parts  of the UI that you can style with CSS  that don't actually exist in your HTML  markup take for example a form input  that has some placeholder text the  placeholder value is defined as an  attribute on the element so how do we  get ahold of the placeholder to style it  the answer is we target a pseudo element  in our CSS they always have a double  colon to distinguish them from pseudo  classes the difference is that a pseudo  element represents an actual part of the  Dom whereas a pseudo class represents  the state of an element based on the  user's interaction with it pseudo  elements can make your code much more  efficient take for example this HTML  where the first letter in each paragraph  is being wrapped in a span so it can be  styled a far more efficient way to solve  this problem is by targeting the first  letter on the paragraph element the end  result is much cleaner semantic HTML  that's cool but by far the most powerful  pseudo elements are before and after  many but not all HTML elements can have  content inserted before or after their  inner content handled entirely from your  CSS code for example we can target  before and provide an empty string as  the content property now that we've  targeted this element you'll notice that  it shows up in the browser's inspector  we can style this element as if it were  an empty span or div in our HTML and  that can be very useful for creating  advanced animations without polluting  your HTML markup imagine we have a big  unordered list and before each list item  we have an emoji character instead of  duplicating it in the markup we can  simply add it as the content property to  the before selector and once again we're  left with code that is far more  maintainable there's a few other pseudo  elements that you should know about  selection allows you to change the  background and color of highlighted text  in some browsers like chrome but not  Firefox you can customize the scroll bar  on scrollable elements before using the  pseudo element always check for browser  compatibility ones like spelling error  might sound cool but they don't really  have any browser support yet this has  been pseudo elements in 100 seconds make  sure to also check out pseudo classes  thanks for watching and I will see you  in the next one  you
 

